<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iliad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iliad is an ancient Greek epic poem attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, composed probably in the eighth or seventh century BCE. Set in the tenth year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, it tells of the wrath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> and its consequences, ending with the funeral of Hector. Its Catalogue of Ships names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> first among the Cretan cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idomeneus">Idomeneus</a>, and the poem became the foundation of Greek education and literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iliad.jpg" alt="Iliad" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Homer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illiad_fragmented_papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Iliad is one of two major surviving ancient Greek epic poems attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, the other being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>. It is divided into 24 books, is written in dactylic hexameter and has 15,693 lines in its standard edition. It was likely composed in Homeric Greek, a literary mixture of Ionic Greek and other dialects, in the late 8th or early 7th century BCE, and its reflection in art and literature shows that it existed before 630 BCE. Its composition, historicity and authorship are extensively debated as the Homeric Question, as is whether the poems were composed independently and whether they survived through an oral or also a written tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem is set towards the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, a ten-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> by a coalition of Mycenaean Greek states, and centres on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, whose wrath, mēnis, the poem's first word, drives the narrative. After nine years of fighting, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, takes the captive Chryseis, and Achilles takes Briseis. Agamemnon's refusal to return Chryseis to her father, a priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, brings a plague on the Achaeans, and when Agamemnon seizes Briseis, Achilles withdraws from the fighting.</p><p>Patroclus then fights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>'s armour and is killed, and Achilles returns to battle and kills the Trojan prince Hector. The poem does not depict the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> or mention the Trojan Horse, which come from later sources. The Olympian gods aid favoured warriors and cities and intervene in personal disputes, and its themes include glory, fate and pride. In the last book Achilles and Priam share food and grief and agree a truce of twelve days for Hector's burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> esteemed the Iliad highly and used it and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> as the basis of education. Professional reciters known as rhapsodes performed it at festivals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a>. By the end of the fifth century BCE both poems were widely known, and in the late fifth century it was the sign of a man of standing to be able to recite them by heart.</p><p>Later writers drew on it: Shakespeare used it as a source for Troilus and Cressida, and George Chapman published his English translation in instalments from 1598, in a long-line ballad metre that quickly became a classic of English poetry. More than 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iliad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Iliad</b> the Phrygians, 'the people of Otreus and godlike Mygdon', fight for Troy from a homeland on the Sangarius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek memory linked the city to its Bronze Age past: the <b>Iliad</b> names Knossos first among the Cretan cities led by Idomeneus, and the Odyssey calls it the great city where Minos ruled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Iliad</b> and the Odyssey of Homer, composed around the 8th or 7th century BCE, show the gods taking part in the Trojan War and the wanderings of Odysseus, intervening in human affairs, favouring their heroes and quarrelling on Olympus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek literature begins with two of its greatest works, the <b>Iliad</b> and the Odyssey, attributed to Homer and composed around 700 BCE from a long tradition of oral poetry.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The best-documented kingdom is Pylos in Messenia, whose palace, found by Carl Blegen in 1939, is called the Palace of Nestor after the old king of the <b>Iliad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In Homer’s <b>Iliad</b> the prize for the footrace at the funeral games of Patroclus is a silver mixing bowl made by Sidonian craftsmen and carried over the sea by Phoenician traders.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
